1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,367
Five point nine million French presses just got recalled. The fix? A plastic spoon.

2
00:00:06,537 --> 00:00:11,130
Glass rarely breaks where it's thin. It breaks where it's scratched.

3
00:00:11,300 --> 00:00:16,654
A metal spoon's hard edge scrapes the inside of the glass as you stir.

4
00:00:16,824 --> 00:00:21,492
It leaves a scratch too fine to see, ending in a razor-sharp tip.

5
00:00:21,662 --> 00:00:26,712
Press the plunger, and the coffee pushes outward. The glass wall stretches.

6
00:00:26,882 --> 00:00:31,910
Picture that stretch as lines running through the glass. The scratch cuts them.

7
00:00:32,080 --> 00:00:37,137
They crowd around its tip, so the pull there gets many times stronger.

8
00:00:37,307 --> 00:00:41,808
Glass can't bend to soften that peak, so the tip creeps deeper.

9
00:00:41,978 --> 00:00:47,309
Rinse it cold while it's still hot, and the inside shrinks first. Another pull.

10
00:00:47,479 --> 00:00:54,661
One day it runs around the wall, and out pours water at ninety-three Celsius, two hundred Fahrenheit.

11
00:00:54,831 --> 00:01:01,005
Seventy reported breaks, forty-eight burns. So owners get a free plastic spoon.

12
00:01:01,175 --> 00:01:06,532
Plastic is softer than glass. It can't cut the tip that starts a crack.

13
00:01:06,702 --> 00:01:12,602
Cogwise: how things work. Follow for the next one!
